Yes. The “create / update / rotate / supersede” decision belongs in Callisto’s release-PR domain—not scattered through a Bash action.

But it cannot be made from release artifacts alone: whether to create or update depends on external facts such as open PRs, branch heads, base SHA, labels, and permissions. The right split is:

```text
Callisto release plan + GitHub snapshot
                 │
                 ▼
       callisto release-pr decide
                 │
                 ▼
  { create | update | rotate | supersede | noop,
    reason, expected state, intended mutations }
                 │
                 ▼
 GitHub Action executes exactly that decision
```

The action should only:
- authenticate and collect the GitHub snapshot;
- invoke Callisto;
- execute the returned mutation request;
- re-check expected state before mutation to handle races;
- emit the decision/report.

Callisto should own:
- managed-branch identity and naming;
- which PRs are eligible;
- ambiguity handling;
- branch rotation policy;
- idempotency/fingerprint rules;
- `create` vs `update` vs `supersede` semantics;
- a typed JSON `release-pr-plan` and `release-pr-report`.

Changesets has the same conceptual ownership, but packages it inside its action: it keeps a version PR current and exposes its PR number as an output. Its GitHub integration uses an API-oriented action implementation rather than a shell workflow rediscovering policy. [Changesets action](https://github.com/changesets/action/blob/main/version/README.md), [Changesets automation model](https://github.com/changesets/changesets/blob/main/docs/automating-changesets.md)

Knope is even clearer: `PrepareRelease` computes and stages the release changes, then an explicit `CreatePullRequest` workflow step creates the PR using configured forge information. That is a domain workflow, not incidental shell branching. [Knope release-preview recipe](https://knope.tech/recipes/1-preview-releases-with-pull-requests/)

PR #45 fixes today’s concrete CLI compatibility incident. We should follow it with a proper Callisto release-PR decision model, so the action becomes an executor rather than a second, fragile release engine.

